*This project has been created as part of the 42 curriculum by peiyli, yopeng.*

# webserv

## Description

**webserv** is an HTTP/1.1 web server written from scratch in C++98, inspired by NGINX. The goal of the project is to gain a deep understanding of the HTTP protocol and of non-blocking, event-driven network programming, by building a server capable of handling multiple simultaneous client connections without ever blocking or forking per request.

The server:

- Parses an NGINX-style configuration file (listen ports, server names, routes, error pages, client body size limits, etc.)
- Handles the **GET**, **POST**, and **DELETE** methods
- Serves static websites, supports file uploads, and generates directory listings (autoindex)
- Ships with an interactive test homepage (served by the default configuration) that lets testers try out every supported method directly from the browser
- Executes **CGI** scripts (e.g., PHP or Python) for dynamic content
- Uses a single `poll()` (or equivalent) loop to multiplex all I/O — reads, writes, and new connections — making it fully non-blocking
- Returns accurate HTTP status codes and default error pages, and remains available under stress

### Project structure

```
webserv/
├── Makefile
├── include/        # Header files
├── src/
│   ├── main.cpp    # Entry point
│   ├── Config.cpp  # Configuration file parsing
│   ├── Server.cpp  # Socket setup and event loop
│   ├── Client.cpp  # Per-connection state management
│   ├── Request.cpp # HTTP request parsing
│   ├── Response.cpp# HTTP response building
│   ├── Router.cpp  # Route matching and method dispatch
│   ├── CGI.cpp     # CGI execution
│   └── Utils.cpp   # Helper functions
└── conf/           # Example configuration files
```

## Instructions

### Requirements

- A C++ compiler supporting C++98 (`c++`, `g++`, or `clang++`)
- `make`
- Python 3 (for CGI scripts)
- A Unix-like system (Linux or macOS)

### Compilation

```bash
git clone <repository_url> webserv
cd webserv
make
```

Available Makefile rules:

| Rule     | Effect                                        |
|----------|-----------------------------------------------|
| `make`   | Compile the project and produce `./webserv`   |
| `make clean`  | Remove object files                      |
| `make fclean` | Remove object files and the executable   |
| `make re`     | Full rebuild                             |

The binary is compiled with `-Wall -Wextra -Werror -std=c++98`.
### Execution

```bash
# With a configuration file
./webserv config/default.conf

# Without arguments (uses config/default.conf by default)
./webserv
```


### Testing

Once the server is running, you can test it with a browser at `http://localhost:8080/` or with `curl`:

```bash
# GET request
curl -v http://localhost:8080/
# POST request
curl -v -X POST -d "hello" http://localhost:8080/upload
# DELETE request
curl -v -X DELETE http://localhost:8080/file.txt
```

Stress testing can be done with tools such as `siege`:

```bash
siege -b http://localhost:8080/
```

## Resources

### Documentation & references

- [RFC 9112 — HTTP/1.1](https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc9112) — the core specification of the HTTP/1.1 protocol
- [RFC 3875 — The Common Gateway Interface (CGI) 1.1](https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc3875) — reference for CGI environment variables and behavior
- [Beej's Guide to Network Programming](https://beej.us/guide/bgnet/) — classic introduction to sockets programming in C
- [NGINX documentation](https://nginx.org/en/docs/) — reference for configuration file syntax and server behavior
- [MDN — An overview of HTTP](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTTP/Overview) — accessible overview of the protocol
- `man` pages: `socket(2)`, `bind(2)`, `listen(2)`, `accept(2)`, `poll(2)`, `fcntl(2)`, `execve(2)`, `pipe(2)`

### AI usage

AI (Claude, Anthropic) was used as a learning and productivity aid during this project, specifically for:

- **Understanding concepts**: clarifying parts of the HTTP/1.1 RFC (chunked transfer encoding, header parsing rules) and the behavior of `poll()`-based event loops
- **Build system**: explaining Makefile mechanics (pattern rules, automatic dependency generation with `-MMD -MP`, order-only prerequisites)
- **Test drafting**: drafting some relative tests according to the requirements

All core source code (configuration parsing, event loop, request/response handling, CGI execution) was designed, written, and debugged by the project's author(s). AI-generated explanations were used for understanding, and any AI-assisted content was reviewed, tested, and validated before inclusion.